  "live_records": {
    "q-37107": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37107",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & -1 \\\\ b & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of  $\\frac{b}{a}.$",
      "worked_solution": "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$\n\nSo, the  first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
